## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical asynchronous processing, per-tenant isolation, idempotency, and multi-env deployment in a beginner-friendly Azure setup.

## Key Concepts

- HTTP-triggered Functions
- Azure Service Bus
- JWT-based tenantId extraction
- Idempotency keys and Redis deduplication
- Per-tenant storage isolation
- GitHub Actions multi-env

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: extract tenant, deduplicate, enqueue
async function processRequest(req) {
  const token = req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
  const tenantId = decodeJWT(token).tenantId;
  const idem = req.headers['x-idempotency-key'] ?? require('uuid').v4();
  if (await redis.exists(`idem:${tenantId}:${idem}`)) throw new Error('Duplicate');
  await redis.set(`idem:${tenantId}:${idem}`, '1', 'EX', 86400);
  const msg = { tenantId, ...req.body, id: uuid() };
  await serviceBus.send(queue, msg);
  return { status: 202 };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency under retries?
- How would you monitor cross-tenant latency and failures?

## Why This Is Asked
The task tests basic serverless HTTP handling, per-tenant isolation, and simple rate-limiting logic using Azure Redis Cache. It touches deployment concerns (bindings, configuration) and error handling in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Functions HTTP trigger in JavaScript/TypeScript
- Redis INCR with TTL-based rate limiting
- Per-tenant isolation and validation
- Basic error handling and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.params.tenantId || req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (!tenantId) return { status: 400, body: 'Missing tenantId' };
  // validate allowlist (omitted for brevity)
  const key = `rate:${tenantId}:count`;
  const count = await redis.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.expire(key, 1);
  if (count > 1) return { status: 429, headers: { 'Retry-After': '1' }, body: '{"error":"Rate limit"}' };
  return { status: 200, body: '{"message":"Hello, '+tenantId+'!"}' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this rate limiter under concurrent requests?
- How would you adapt this for bursty traffic and distributed tenants?

